# prime-powered

**A computational companion I'm building to my own research paper.**

![python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.10%2B-blue)
![license](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-lightgrey)
![status](https://img.shields.io/badge/status-in%20progress-yellow)

> A. Bhardwaj, A. Boyer-Paulet, W. Hindes, E. Qiu, A. Sun, *Prime-powered images and irreducible polynomials in dynamical semigroups*, [arXiv:2510.10310](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10310) — accepted at *Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. Ser. B*.

## Status

- **`dynamics.py` — working.** Brute-force search and classification for Theorem 2.1, plus the sharpness search.
- **`irreducibility.py` — in progress.** Section 3 (building irreducible polynomials in the semigroup). Stub only for now.

I'm building this myself, module by module, alongside coursework at Yale.

## What it does

The paper proves a classification theorem (Theorem 2.1): if iterating `f(x) = x^d + c` enough times lands on a prime power, the starting point has to be preperiodic. I didn't want to only trust the proof by hand, so `dynamics.py` checks it directly:

- `search_theorem` finds every solution of `f^N(α) = ε·y^p` in a window by brute force
- `statement` checks each one against the theorem's four cases by computing orbits, not by using the proof
- `search_near_misses` looks one iterate early to find the examples showing the bound can't be lowered

## Results so far

| d | maps (c) | \|α\| ≤ | prime-power hits | mismatches |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 1,200 | 40 | 33 | 0 |
| 3 | 600 | 25 | 0 | 0 |
| 4 | 400 | 12 | 5 | 0 |
| 5 | 200 | 8 | 0 | 0 |
| 6 | 120 | 6 | 10 | 0 |

2,520 maps, 48 hits, every one matches the theorem. Runs in about a second. The sharpness search recovers the paper's example `x² − 460`, where `f³(22) = 114²` but 22 isn't preperiodic.

## Run it

    pip install sympy
    python -c "import dynamics as D; r = D.verify_theorem(2, range(-600, 601), 40); print(len(r['solutions']), 'hits,', len(r['violations']), 'mismatches')"

